      "writtenOn": "2026-01-29T14:24:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is a big shift in how the test expectation data and validation code is written. By defining in terms of 16 bit units, we can easily write for 32-bit values, 16-bit values, and layouts that still treat half floats as 32-bits.\n\nFor the existing float-only test cases, the expectations basically replicate each symbol, e.g. `{1,2,_,_}` becomes `{1,1,2,2,_,_,_,_}` (\"float\" values push their bit pattern into the high and low 16-bit word). For the new half-only test cases, if the layout does not support f16 values, the 32-bit float is differentiated from the full float test by encoding a float value that has the bit pattern in just the low 16-bit word (so instead of `1,1` we\u0027d expect `1,_`). For half-only tests where the layout *does* support f16 values, we can simply expect `1`.",
